::::::::::::::: columns
::::: {.column width="25%"}
::: {style="font-size:30px"}
**Descriptive**
:::

::: {style="font-size:20px"}
Sum up the information of the data set.

First kind of analysis to do, understand data composition. We will need statistics to try to generalize the information obtained.
:::
:::::

::::: {.column width="25%"}
::: {style="font-size:30px"}
**Diagnostic**
:::

::: {style="font-size:20px"}
Understand relations between data, reasons behind changes.

If we introduce statistics we can do inferential analysis working with a sample and extract information.
:::
:::::

::::: {.column width="25%"}
::: {style="font-size:30px"}
**Predictive**
:::

::: {style="font-size:20px"}
Use existing data to predict more data never seen before.

The capability of predict X with Y doesn't mean that Y is the cause of X. Prediction is not casualty.
:::
:::::

::::: {.column width="25%"}
::: {style="font-size:30px"}
**Prescriptive**
:::

::: {style="font-size:20px"}
Guide decision making with the help of rest of analysis.

Related with causal analysis, to identify what happens to a variable with the change of another.
:::
:::::
